#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084368} :activity/id #uuid "57664c41-15bf-58d4-8c3a-0a863ff5156d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050669 :topic/id #uuid "5d821651-a7cb-50af-8f0d-bc162e3a74e7" :topic/math-academy-id 729} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084468 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the sum and the product of the solutions of the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 160x - 6\\,882 = 0\n$$\n\nOne way is to find the roots and then compute their sum and product. However, this method might be cumbersome because some of the coefficients of our quadratic equation are large.\n\nAn alternative method is to use **Vieta's formulas.** Vieta's formulas state that, for a quadratic equation of the form\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwith $a \\neq 0$ and two solutions $x=x_1$ and $x=x_2,$ the sum of these solutions is\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = -\\dfrac{b}{a},\n$$\n\nand the product of these solutions is\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nWe'll explain how these formulas are derived at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1bacc29-6f0e-5c99-aab3-8a77154a17d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4442 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5898a8e1-42dd-5ff7-9540-b7ded63e6b27" :step/math-academy-id 476832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084408 :tutorial/content "Let's find the sum and product of the solutions of our equation \n\n$$\n2x^2 - 160x - 6\\,882 = 0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\na=2,\\qquad b=-160,\\qquad c=-6\\,882.\n$$\n\nApplying Vieta's formulas, we get the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-160)}{2} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6\\,882)}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\,441.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's check our results. First, note that the equation can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+31)(x-111) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product rule, the solutions to this equation are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -31, \\qquad x_2 = 111.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = -31 + 111 = 80\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nand the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = -31 \\cdot 111 = -3\\,441. \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c978ee52-e1ec-57b7-8779-be1d9aaa0ea9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13437 :tutorial/title "Applying Vieta's Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "cd57ada6-8f57-54fc-9dad-877823f35b12" :step/math-academy-id 476833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083387 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082953 :question/id #uuid "9ad6a79c-424b-5b92-9c82-06093a8a500f" :question/math-academy-id "e-18862"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03456ac4-5798-56cc-8c14-0a645b2e232e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049077}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541102}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sum of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "b284a203-5729-53b0-91fe-17848cb4aaaf" :step/math-academy-id 476834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082954 :question/id #uuid "3c05b35d-f928-5fb9-924e-f1780dc0420d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18863"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390572}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "891681b6-a3ea-5b4d-ada0-57c4b3e0c306" :step/math-academy-id 476837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083389 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082955 :question/id #uuid "3c0ff6f6-74b4-5e47-b524-5a7c6f0b6400" :question/math-academy-id "e-18864"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18c41022-916d-5d5c-89e6-ebfdc56f8fe3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541111}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Quadratic Equation With a Given Sum and Product of Roots"} :step/id #uuid "9f44dc8e-a776-5448-8a44-d86721c6c70b" :step/math-academy-id 476840 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084469 :tutorial/content "We'll now derive Vieta's formulas for the sum and product of the solutions of a quadratic equation.\n\nConsider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $x_1$ and $x_2.$ \n\nAccording to a mathematical theorem known as the fundamental theorem of algebra, the left-hand side of our quadratic equation can *always* be factored as \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = a(x-x_1)(x-x_2).\n$$\n\n \n\nYou'll learn more about the fundamental theorem of algebra in future lessons.\n\nExpanding the parentheses on the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) & =a({x}^{2}-{x}_{1}x-{x}_{2}x+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =a({x}^{2}-({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})x+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}-a({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})x+a{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is identical to the original equation, we have\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = ax^2 - a(x_1 + x_2) x + ax_1x_2\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing both sides by $a,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{a}}x+{\\color{red}\\dfrac{c}{a}} = x^2 \\:{\\color{blue}- \\:(x_1+x_2)}x + {\\color{red}x_1 x_2}.\n$$\n\nSince this equation is true for all values of $x,$ we can extract Vieta's formulas by equating the coefficients of the linear and constant terms on both sides of the equation, as follows:\n\n- Equating the linear coefficients, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{b}{a}}} = \\color{blue}-(x_1+x_2)\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = -\\dfrac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\nx_1 x_2 = \\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5c730ee6-d995-5604-bb74-413a8e7845ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4443 :tutorial/title "Deriving Vieta's Formulas"} :step/id #uuid "1ff512cc-3603-556c-a912-90faade997df" :step/math-academy-id 476843}] :activity/title "Vieta's Formulas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083424}]}
